> take key
The key is colder than the room around it.

> take coat
You take the linen coat.

> polish key
You polish the key with the cuff of the linen coat until brass shows through the dark.

> east
Study

Dusty shelves lean over a desk scarred by old candle wax. A black ledger waits beneath the lamp.

You can see a black ledger.

Available exits: west.

Rain moves across the glass in a pattern almost like handwriting.

> read ledger
The ledger lists owners in a narrow hand. The final entry has no name, only a water stain shaped like a key.

Somewhere in the hall, the caretaker turns a page you cannot see.
!flag ledger_read

> west
Foyer

Rain needles the glass around the front door. Study dust has found the cracks in the tile.

You can see a hall table.

Available exits: east, north.

> unlock box
The polished key turns once in the wooden box. The lock gives up with a small domestic click.

> open box
You open the wooden box. A folded rain-soft note waits inside.

> take note from box
You take the folded note.

> read note
The note says: When the glass forgets the garden, look for the pane that remembers rain.
!flag rain_note_read

> north
Hall

The hall is long and glass-framed. Rain sketches the names from the ledger against the panes.

You can see a caretaker.

Available exits: east, south, west.

The floorboards answer your first step with a tired creak.

> east
Conservatory

Glass ribs hold the rain above ranks of empty planters. The cracked pane stands out now, a narrow wound in the careful room.

Available exits: west.

The conservatory air is warmer than the hall and much less alive.

The rain repeats the note's rhythm against the glass.

> look
Conservatory

Glass ribs hold the rain above ranks of empty planters. The cracked pane stands out now, a narrow wound in the careful room.

Available exits: west.

With the rain note in mind, you spot the cracked pane behind the empty planters.
!contains cracked pane

> read pane
The crack is not lettering, but your eye keeps making a word from it: safe.
!flag safe_means_trapped

> use pane
You ease the cracked pane inward. Behind it, a narrow stair climbs through the wall of glass. You climb into the upper house.
!room upstairs_landing

> unlock door
The polished key turns in the bedroom door with the reluctance of an old apology.

> open door
You open the bedroom door. Air slips out smelling of dust, linen, and rain that never reached the floor.

> north
Locked Bedroom

A narrow bed sits made and untouched. A covered mirror hangs over the washstand.

You can see a covered mirror.

Available exits: south.

> read mirror
There is no writing on the mirror, but the dust along the sheet has settled into a line: let the room change.
!flag mirror_message_read

> use mirror
You touch the mirror where the Glass Room waits in reflection. The surface gives way like cold water, and the room receives you without a sound.
!room glass_room
!scene glass_room

> read open ledger
The page lists no owner. It only repeats one phrase in a narrowing hand: kept safe, kept safe, kept safe.
!flag glass_ledger_read

> write name
You write your name on the blank page. The ink does not trap you; it opens like a window left unlatched.
!flag ending_written_name
!chapter after_rain
!scene house_remembers_you

> undo
Undone.
!scene glass_room

> leave open ledger
You leave the ledger open. The room begins to forget by letting the rain touch each perfect thing.
!flag ending_left_open
!chapter after_rain
!scene house_forgetting
